---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import "../../styles/museum.css";
---

<Layout
  title="The Jeep Incident | Museum of Dumb Ideas"
  description="The story of a 15-year-old, a Jeep, and the discovery that confidence is not traction."
>
  <div class="museum-page">
    <p class="museum-back">
      <a href="/museum/">← Museum of Dumb Ideas</a>
    </p>

    <header class="exhibit-header">
      <p class="exhibit-number">Accession No. MDI-0002</p>
      <h1>The Jeep Incident</h1>

      <p class="museum-subtitle">
        An early field study confirming that confidence and four-wheel drive
        are not the same thing.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="museum-plaque" aria-label="Exhibit information">
      <p class="plaque-label">Exhibit Information</p>

      <dl>
        <dt>Classification</dt>
        <dd>Questionable Experiment</dd>

        <dt>Year</dt>
        <dd>Circa 1980s</dd>

        <dt>Original Plan</dt>
        <dd>"This'll be fun."</dd>

        <dt>Actual Result</dt>
        <dd>Jeep buried in mud</dd>

        <dt>Recovery Method</dt>
        <dd>Outside assistance</dd>

        <dt>Lesson Learned</dt>
        <dd>Confidence is not traction</dd>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <article class="exhibit-body">
      <h2>Description</h2>

      <p>
        I was 15 when I found a Jeep and, shortly afterward, found enough mud
        to bury it.
      </p>

      <p>
        The plan was not complicated. There was a Jeep, there was mud, and I
        had the kind of confidence generally available only to people who have
        not yet accumulated enough experience to interfere with it.
      </p>

      <p>
        The Jeep went in. It did not come back out.
      </p>

      <h2>Original Hypothesis</h2>

      <blockquote>
        It's a Jeep. It'll be fine.
      </blockquote>

      <h2>What Went Wrong</h2>

      <p>
        The available evidence suggests that I mistook having four-wheel drive
        for having a plan.
      </p>

      <p>
        Forward progress ended, the Jeep settled into the mud, and the
        experiment advanced to the outside-assistance phase. This was not part
        of the original plan, although the original plan had very few parts.
      </p>

      <h2>Photographic Evidence</h2>

      <div class="artifact-gallery">
        <figure>
          <img
            src="/images/museum/jeep-stuck-recovery-underway.jpeg"
            alt="A Jeep buried to its axles in a muddy clearing while two people assess the recovery"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>
            The Jeep, after confidence exceeded available traction and outside
            assistance entered the plan.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure>
          <img
            src="/images/museum/brad-posing-with-stuck-jeep.jpeg"
            alt="Fifteen-year-old Brad posing on the hood of the mud-covered Jeep"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>
            The curator at 15, presenting the results with appropriate
            confidence.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <h2>Artifacts</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>Two surviving photographs.</li>
        <li>One thoroughly buried Jeep.</li>
        <li>A recovery operation requiring people with better judgment.</li>
        <li>A lesson that became funnier after enough time had passed.</li>
      </ul>

      <aside class="curator-note">
        <h2>Curator's Note</h2>

        <p>
          At 15, confidence was standard equipment. Traction was apparently
          optional.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2>Exhibit Rating</h2>

      <div class="rating-grid">
        <article class="rating-card">
          <h3>Confidence</h3>
          <p class="rating-value" aria-label="Five out of five stars">
            ★★★★★
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="rating-card">
          <h3>Planning</h3>
          <p class="rating-value" aria-label="One out of five stars">
            ★☆☆☆☆
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="rating-card">
          <h3>Traction</h3>
          <p class="rating-value" aria-label="One out of five stars">
            ★☆☆☆☆
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="rating-card">
          <h3>Storytelling Value</h3>
          <p class="rating-value" aria-label="Five out of five stars">
            ★★★★★
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <h2>Lessons Learned</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>Confidence is not traction.</li>
        <li>Four-wheel drive does not eliminate consequences.</li>
        <li>Outside assistance is still assistance, even when it was not in the plan.</li>
      </ul>

      <footer class="museum-footer">
        <p>
          The Jeep Incident is on permanent loan from the Archives of
          Adolescent Confidence.
        </p>

        <p>
          <a href="/museum/">Continue through the Museum</a>
          ·
          <a href="/">Return to BradCooke.com</a>
        </p>
      </footer>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>
